Roberto Battiston: Dare to have the courage to go into space.
Space is subject to appeal, dream, danger.
What you see is a small solar phenomenon that happened a few years ago: a small patch issued a burst of particles, plasma, and sun-spot 1692, exactly as big as the Earth.
What happened in the following hours, in the following days: a huge mass of particles, plasma, energy, arrived two days later on Earth and what happened it lit up the skies of Northern Europe with formidable, unprecedented aurora borealis.
But that was all that happened, because the Earth is protected from space phenomena, a hostile space, by at least three things: the magnetic field which deflects the particles, our planet that partly shields us, and the atmosphere, which is like having 2.5 m of aluminum in front of us that protect us from particles.
But if we were travelling to Mars, if we did, what for so many years, since the days of von Brown, we have wanted to do, get to the Red Planet with astronauts, what would protect us?
How would we be able to resist this and all challenges which space offers us?
The space that we have conquered: is the International Space Station, as big as a football field, it can accommodate up to nine astronauts.
It is the technological gem of modern times.
It is something absolutely fantastic.
Samantha Cristoforetti, Luca Parmitano: our astronauts have been there for months housed in this large space hotel in which they managed to survive the hostility of space.
However, during the mission, something unpredictable happened to Luca Parmitano: a small pump that ran the cooling of the space suit with which Parmitano was walking in space, while he was working outside the space station, broke.
It started to leak: half a glass of water which began to float in his helmet, aspirated from his nose and his mouth.
He was about to drown in a glass of water in space.
Who would have imagined that?
Nobody.
It nearly happened.
He saved himself because his comrades took him inside quickly and he was able to free himself from the half glass of water that was drowning him.
What are we missing?
We lack self repairing materials, systems that are able to protect themselves automatically.
Where can we look for this type of new technology?
Nicola Pugno: Nature is, of course, able to self-heal.
We see it every day: when we scratch ourselves, thank heaven, we can repair ourselves.
Even recently, since the 2000s, from a engineering point of view, we are able to mimic this characteristic a bit.
The concept is very simple: you can imagine it by referring to this scenario.
Imagine putting capsules in a traditional material.
These capsules, in turn, contain a sort of glue, as a manner of speaking, on microcapsules, there are many spread a little everywhere.
When a crack propagates and starts to break my structure, it breaks the capsules too, and they release the liquid that will mend the crack, so essentially, we have a material that is self healing.
It is something that already exists.
There are even paints that are able to self-heal, that when scratched they repair temselves.
RB: Amazing, a truly useful technology!
This is part of a more general framework in which we imitate nature.
NP: Certainly!
Imitating nature is something that we've always done.
Think of Leonardo's studies, on flight.
Observing the flight of birds we tried to build the first machines to fly.
It is important to get inspiration, from nature rather than just copy it.
Planes do not flap their wings, but we have achieved remarkable results that we use every day.
RB: Very well, then at this point, it will mean that the next time we have to repair a piece of the space station, look, in this case, a panel almost 80 meters long was that has been unrolled, in one point it showed a crack and the astronaut had to sew and repair it with difficulty and a huge risk.
More resistant materials are needed, that do not break, and can withstand the hostility of space, and be furled and unfurled.
NP: Yeah, certainly, one possibility is for it to autorepair, another possibility is that they do not break.
So for inspiration, the toughest material is the material that has the greatest capacity to dissipate energy before breaking, it is the spider silk.
Spiders are exceptional: to think that any little spider you find around is basically able to make up to seven different types of silk.
Here you see the apparatus for spinning silk
which can produce materials that are extremely resistant or extremely tenacious, or able to dissipate even more energy using structural solutions that we will see later they are also sticky to hold their prey.
They manage to make really incredible things.
To give an idea, this material is ten times tougher than Kevlar.
Here you see a junction in correspondence of the web itself, you see an additional length of silk inside a droplet and this further increases the ability to dissipate the energy of the web structure.
By doing so an insect is caught and so the spider evolved and survives to this day.
RB: The spider is really something extraordinary.
We need very special materials in space.
For example, a helicopter on Mars would need, because of a greatly reduced atmosphere than the Earth one, blades that run much faster, which are much larger and lighter.
Today we are unable to make them: even the strongest materials such as carbon fiber struggle to withstand these particular stresses.
NP: Yes,carbon fiber is actually the strongest fiber that we know today, from an engineering point of view, at a macroscopic scale at least.
Six GigaPascal: six times the strength of steel.
Theoretically there is a material that is even more resistant, at least from an ideal point of view, it's a single layer of carbon atoms: the famous, graphene.
Multiple layers of graphene become graphite which is basically what we have in our pencils, that is not resistant because the layers flow between them, but a single sheet of graphite is extremely durable.
100 times stronger than steel, at least theoretically.
This way we can certainly make the blades of a helicopter to go on Mars and it could have applications on Earth.
Many people couldn't be saved in Nepal, for example, because we hadn't had helicopters that could go below a certain altitude.
But we would be able to do even more extravagant things, such as, a space elevator cable to bring mass from Mars into space at very reduced costs with benefits that we know well because we use elevators.
RB: But if we wanted to go beyond having just a single very strong layer, to have materials with structures that we can use for whole series of applications. What does nature do?
NP: This is, indeed, very important.
We use the carbon fibers within the matrices of other materials to build composite materials.
When we combine materials, problems of defects and other factors, reduce the strength.
Indeed today carbon based composites are not so strong.
But there are composites in nature.
We observed the biological composite material with the greatest strength and we found it in the limpets's shells.
By picking these elements, by trying the traction, we saw a mechanical strength of six GigaPascal, that is much greater than any composite that man is able to make.
RB: Amazing, we are really picking up a lot of input, suggestions and extraordinary cues from nature.
Well, one of the problems we have both in low orbit and in deep space, is that of so called space debris, micrometeorites.
They move so fast between 7, 8, 10 km per second that even a very small object - here we have an example of a centimeter metal ball, that hitting a piece of 18 centimeter thick aluminum has virtually gone through it, it has deformed it due to the huge energy that it dissipated with a modality that the material was unable to absorb by itself.
We would definitely need tougher screens, or barriers.
What can we look at in nature in this respect?
NP: Think of insects.
Nature has evolved absolutely in this context.
The armor of the insects is an example.
Here in particular we see a very interesting beetle, the so-called bombardier beetle, which is able to spray this fluid at high temperature and great pressure to get rid of enemies.
That's why, you may have noticed, I was scared when the drummer emitted this flows, they reminded me a bit of the thing that we see here.
The beetle has this remarkable peculiarity, because imagine the material that makes up the combustion chamber.
You see, it has a very light structure, but it also has this wavy surface and this undulating surface seems to be the key to resisting the explosions, as in this case, but also impacts.
In fact, by doing simulations - you can see a diagram of a numerical simulation in the lower right - we have observed that, for the same mass and the same average thickness of this sheet, if corrugated with a certain ripple the protective capacity of this element becomes much greater.
In the future one could think of making shields for spaceships to Mars inspired by the bombardier beetle.
RB: Amazing, but another problem that we have in space is that we need structures and when we leave with our missiles, by definition, they can only be folded.
One difficulty for space engineering is to be able to open them, possibly to close them, in a reliable way, by taking advantage of the weightlessness, when we are in space, and the flexibility and refolding when we are still departing to Earth.
It is one of the hardest things to make sure that the mechanism opens and it goes back in place as we want.
From this point of view what can nature tell us?
NP: Nature can say, as always, a lot.
Here you see a simple ladybug.
'Very simple' so to speak, 'very common', I would say.
The ladybug manages to spread its wings without any problems, and can refold them.
Imagine the complexity. It folds them thanks to a system of ribs on the wings, which are also structural for the wings, evidently, but with a sort of elastic stability, so to speak, it is able to open and close them in a discontinuous, and very controlled way.
So we could imagine doing something similar to control the opening and the closing of these panels.
RB: This is definitely technology of the third millennium: we need to make an extraordinary leap to go do something that in billions of years nature has already learned and is able to do.
We have arrived on Mars, we have landed: now we need the resources.
The first resource the astronaut needs is water.
We know that on Mars there is water, frozen, under layers of soil, but that there is also an atmosphere.
So there must be some water in the Martian atmosphere.
If we could recover the atmospheric water as we do on Earth it would be extremely useful.
What could we do, taking a cue from nature?
NP: Well, there are organisms that can do this.
Think, for example, of the cactus.
Here I would take a different example: another beetle, a beetle from Namibia, a desert beetle, which has a surface formed by two, hydrophobic and hydrophilic, materials.
That is, materials which, on the one hand, are able to attract water and, on the other hand, allow it to flow very well.
This combination of two types of surfaces within the same macroscopic surface allows it to recover a lot of water, and it manages to survive in desert environments.
RB: Fantastic: beetles that take water from the air.
But, speaking of surfaces, we would need self-cleaning surfaces.
For example, this is one of the rovers that are on the Martian surface.
After a few months, it was covered with dust: solar panels, we see that on the left, are completely unusable.
Fortunately, every now and then, as well as the storms there are lighter winds, small whirlwinds that clean them and, fortunately, fix the problem as seen on the right.
But if we had surfaces that are inherently self-cleaning, that manage to eliminate all forms of deposit, we would certainly be even more prepared to deal with an interplanetary journey.
NP: Self-cleaning surfaces certainly exist in nature; the most famous is the lotus leaf, so that we talk of the lotus effect.
Here you see some water droplets that slide on this surface.
It is quite clear that it is water repellent or super hydrophobic, in this sense, and it is a very special surface, so that it is considered sacred to some people, because, while living in swamps, and other dirty places, it manages to keep very clean.
This is substantially due to surface topology invisible to the naked eye, but well noted under the microscope.
A topology thanks to which, the material goes from just a bit hydrophobic from a chemical point of view to super hydrophobic.
It is therefore clear that by mimicking this topology, as we can with today's nanotechnology, we can effectively change the properties of 'wetability', that is, the interaction of the surface with water.
Here you can see a plastic surface inspired by the lotus leaf that we made, simply with this topology invisible to the naked eye, the interaction with the fluid changes dramatically.
RB: Exceptional!
One of the thing we have seen, an imitations of nature is the famous Velcro.
Do you remember, it was patented and studied because someone asked themselves why, when you walk in the fields, the seeds of some plants stick to the socks.
Velcro is used everywhere in space to attach and close things.
Even the suits that go to the moon have pieces of Velcro allow quick closure.
But on this subject nature has done much more, right?
NP: Velcro is extremely interesting.
The main problem of the velcro is that it will stick one item to another, that is, I can attach hooks to the small rings that are located there.
In nature, of course, something much more sophisticated was necessary.
The most sophisticated animal, from this point of view, because it is also the biggest, and so it had to evolve itself with the best adhesive mechanisms, is the gecko, whose paw is covered with billions of practically two-dimensional structures, each of which exerts a minute force that added together make the very great adhesive strength of the gecko.
To give you an idea, it is five times stronger than an ideal sucker.
It also manages to control this adhesion; this is perhaps the most intriguing thing.
It can unstick its foot very easily, using the technique we use when we do not want to hurt ourselves taking off a band aid that is, changing the angle at which it removes the setae from the surface.
You could think of surfaces inspired by the gecko to go wherever we want.
RB: We have to tell Spiderman,
that he have to become Geckoman to go into space. Well, there is a duration and exposition to radiation problem and difficulties related to the deep vacuum, the heat and the cold, and nature gave us extraordinary indications in this respect too.
Both brewers yeast, lichens streptococci, and some more complex structures such as plankton, and tardigrades which are small animals, but nevertheless complex, were sent to space, exposed to the radiation, to the vacuum, and the temperature changes. They went into a kind of hibernation and then were awakened after 10, 15, 20 months, discovering that some of these individuals were still alive.
It's amazing what nature can do, for it has never been exposed to these conditions, yet, some of these animals on Earth have evidently managed to develop these characteristics.
But there is a challenge unwinnable: the duration of a space trip!
Mars 6.8 months, but for the nearest star 100,000 years with today's launch and travel techniques.
How can we expect to face such a long journey?
NP: Well, here we can only hope to get inspiration, but It's a tough challenge.
It's worth mentioning that there is only one organism known to be immortal.
A jellyfish which is called the immortal jellyfish, that has the capacity to rejuvenate, that is return to the state of polyp in the toughest conditions.
So it would be like a man in unfavorable conditions, who decides to return to being a child and then have another go at life when extreme, too challenging conditions, would have disappeared.
It is certainly a very interesting ability but difficult to mimic.
RB: Now I understand why some science fiction movies show giant jellyfish arriving in spaceships, they are clearly informed of this possibility.
In any case it is clear that interplanetary travel, possibly one day when we have more effective ways to move, interstellar travel requires completely different technologies to which we are not accustomed and that nature could give us, as you saw, extremely interesting stimuli and challenges.
Probably tomorrow we will need new technologies.
That's why the challenge of going to Mars is so interesting to improve the whole range of man's technical capacities.
NP: We can not end like this: when will we go to Mars?
RB: I do not know, but I'm sure that the man and the woman who will go there have already been born.
Thank you.
(Applause)
I work in the digital economy in Europe and Italy, and I had the luck of spending part of my career in California-based companies.
I've been an angel investor for many years ago now, a start-up investor.
2017 for me was a year filled with professional satisfactions: I was awarded twice for my activity as angel investor, one in Italy and one in Europe. In the last two years, I've been in the "Inspiring 50", the 50 more inspirational women in the technological field in Europe.
I guess I should be in heaven.
But I'm not.
For me, 2017 has been, so far, a year of defeat for women in the field of technology.
Those defeats hurt us, because they come from the same place where innovation and future belong; a place we all take a model, where everything that happens ripples around the whole world.
I mean, Silicon Valley.
In 2017, a number of events and behaviours came about that are disturbing, both for their nature and for the silence around them.
In February 2017, Susan Fowler shared her experience as a developer targeted with unwanted, rejected sexual attentions among a team of Uber engineers, and she set a process in motion that brought Travis Kalanick, Uber's CEO, to resign on the 20th Of June.
The 13th of June, in an Uber meeting, businesswoman Arianna Huffington, a member in the board of directors, shared her desire to involve more women in the board of directors.
David Bonderman, her colleague, interrupted her with a sexist joke: "More women, more gossip", he said.
The reaction forced Bonderman to resign from his position in the Uber board.
On June 30th, the New York Times published a report where more than two dozen of aspiring businesswomen, including Rachel Renock, reported sexist episodes during their interactions with venture capital fund managers whom they offered to invest in their companies.
In July, Dave McClure, founder of the "500 startups" incubator, resigned after confessing improper behaviours with business women in search for funds, associates and candidates.
Among them, Sara Kunst, who had told her story to the New York Times.
A memo came out in August, written by James Damore, a Google employee, blamed the company's efforts in diversity, claiming that women are biologically inappropriate to work as developer.
The 7th of August, Google fired him; and he quickly became a spokesperson of a misogynous, alt-right way of thought in the Silicon Valley.
In the past, California brought about a number of wonderful things: cultural exports like James Dean, Marilyn Monroe, John Wayne, the Beach Boys, the beat generation, the hippy culture, the Black Panther, punk music, Ronald Reagan, Blade Runner, the Mac, Indiana Jones, the iPhone.
This year, we had nothing but poison between men and women.
Plenty of poison.
I'm going to go back to the beginning of the '90s.
At that time, as a newly graduated, I started my career in a multinational company focused on strategic advice, in Milan.
I didn't have many female role models, we can actually say I only had one: Marisa Bellisario, who graduated in BA in the '90s.
She worked as a developer on the first Olivetti mainframes, then she moved to the commercial sector in Olivetti and in General Electric.
In 1981 she became CEO of Italtel, a big company in huge crisis which she successfully turned around in a few years.
She found her way to the cover of worldwide magazines, she stood out for her hairstyles and her outfits. And it's largely acknowleged, she'd move further in her career hadn't she received a veto by some powerful men.
In 1988, at the age of 53, she died for a bone tumour.
So I started my career with a single female role model.
And she wasn't even alive, she was already dead.
Some time ago, I wondered what the people who started their career with me, in that same place and year,
were doing now. More than half of men are CEO and general director; managing director; senior partner; managing partner; chief strategy officer.
But not women.
Women do other things, with a few hopeful exceptions.
They took other paths.
I made my choice: I am an independent advisor and an angel investor, as I told you.
During my career, I had responsibilities and many satisfactions.
Today I am independent, a freelance: I make public speeches about digital economy, startups and innovation; I can witness the success of women manager and I can talk about my work, in particular, in Italy and abroad.
But I'm not in power.
Yes, I have many responsibilities, but I only exert my share of collective power in the boards I belong.
I am independent, a freelance.
The same is true for many colleagues I met during my career.
So, what happened to the women of my generation?
What was put between them and those goals they had at the beginning of the '90s, and what can we do so that future generations won't go through what we had to?
So that other women can reach their goals without hitting a wall?
And what is the price we pay for this situation?
It is well-known that world economy, our shared wealth, pays a high price, in terms of missing growth, for the lack of women in decision-making processes in the business world; according to OECD, we are about to lose two and a half point of growth in the next eight years, due to the partecipation gap.
By halving that gap, the world would grow more.
In companies, the lack of diversity in management, is linked with a decreased degree of innovation compared to the one of companies where at least the 20% of top managers are women.
But these economic topics are well-known, and trigger no change, sadly.
So I'd rather give you some data on the human cost of this situation. These costs should concern not only women, but also men, those men who have, in the majority of the cases, powerful roles in companies, while women have other roles.
We have real evidence, for example, on how separating manhood and the role of head of the family, or breadwinner, could have important benefits for women and men.
An American study shows us that if salaries in a couple shift, over time, in favour of the man, the well-being of this man is more likely to deteriorate: anxiety and depression increase.
The effect is even worse if the man remains the only one, in the couple, to earn money.
A longitudinal study on 72,000 Swedish men showed that decades after the birth of a child fathers who took advantage of a parental leave for one or two months survived longer than those who didn't, all else - age, education and job position - being equal.
The mortality rate of men who took care of their children was 24% lower than among those men who didn't do it.
Fathers' experience of taking care for babies seems to prevent risky behaviours, especially alcoholism; and the effect lasts for many decades after.
What fosters equality at work and at home?
Equality in economic opportunities and caring duties?
There is no silver bullet, unfortunately; we can just make hypotheses.
A study on 6,000 Danish companies with a male CEO showed that when his firstborn is female, the gap in salaries in the company decreses because women's salaries grow quicker than men's ones.
This is not the case if the firstborn is male.
An American study claims that venture capital funds where partners have daughters hire more women.
And not only they hire more women, they also perform better.
Obviously, asking CEOs and top managers to have more daughters isn't a viable policy lever; we cannot force top managers to reproduce and generate these strange creatures of the opposite sex.
But maybe we can advice young women at their first job: before sending that resume, it may be worthy to consider the familiar structure of those who are in charge.
What does this mean?
That gender stereotypes, which exist and are before our eyes, do not hurt just women.
Stereotypes are cages: wider and more comfortable for men; narrower, more limiting for women, and one even narrower for transgender, intersex and binary people.
Stereotypes hurt everyone of us.
They hurt us women, who accept being seen as the queens of multitasking and leaving most of the power, too much of it, to men.
As a consequence, we still suffer a salary gap, our career is slower, we have less money and less saving, we are less independent.
And the prejudice against us seems to endure, both in millennials and in their parents' generation.
But stereotypes hurt men too, because manhood can become a cage.
Since childhood, we treat boys like incomplete children who can only express a limited range of emotions.
If a girl loves football or cars, we now know it's okay, we let her be a tomboy.
But if a boy loves ballet, we still treat him like Billy Elliot in the '80s.
We raise these boys in their manhood stereotypes cage.
They go to school, then they start working and we expect them to be their family's economic engine.
With their stereotypes, and ours.
When a woman quits her job to take care of her children, which sadly happens more often than it should, she is completely supported.
We say: what a good mother, what a heroine, look at the sacrifices she made to raise her children.
In the few cases, instead, when men make that decision, staying at home with sons, maybe because his partner makes more than him, we punish him, deem him weak and inappropriate.
This double standard is extremely dangerous.
Stereotypes hurt everybody: women, men, the whole society.
I have the feeling, elsewhere women are moving on quicker than us in the West.
In China, for example, by contrasting stereotypes, 55% of digital startups are founded by women.
How many are here?
In China, women are partners in 80% of venture capital funds.
They decide where to invest.
They are in march, they're moving on: here, in the West, we feel kind of stuck, or going forward too slowly - and, sometimes, going back.
Even in innovative contexts, where our future is driven, women and men, in the work environment, seem to be separated by a huge canyon.
And the summer of poison I talked about came from this very canyon.
It seems a defeat, there's a disappointment for trying to win without considering how rigged the game was.
It is enough to get me giving up dialog, staying among peers, lock ourselves up among those who understand us, and cut every bridge.
I am sure, instead, that the time for building bridges, more than ever, is now: bridges between women and men for us to meet halfway and shake our hands.
Let's make an effort together: it's time to challenge the canyon between women and men.
What does this mean?
I don't want to repeat recipes you already know, like the need, for companies, to implement a different work organization; or the volountary effort to lean-in, which is, most of the times, something women do.
I have three new recipes for you, which all deal with building bridges: one is personal, one is for media and one is political.
The first one is personal: hygiene, in the work environment, is your responsibility.
I'm talking about behavioural hygiene: if you're a man, you have to know a lot of things happen every day at work: interruptions, jokes, comments whispered words, ideas thought by someone and attributed to someone else, that hurt a woman like a thousand cuts with a paper sheet's border.
Something that you, men, often don't even realize.
Sit down with a woman and ask her, if and when it happens, to notice you, while it happens, in a careful and effective way.
Maybe with a gesture, a glance, a signal you had decided.
Take then aside the men who interrupted, made fun of, excluded or dimished a woman and explain them that this, in 2017, is not acceptable.
If instead you're a woman, don't deplete your energies uselessly by holding your frustration, or by trying to explain these things by yourself.
Find a male ally, build a bridge with him, and have him work at it.
The second recipe is for the media: let's critically reconsider the messages around us.
Your children's school books are full of stereotypes, even today, in 2017.
Mums and dad, look at these books.
Not just mums.
Talk about them with teachers, and make them choose books which are appropriate to our time.
Television, advertisment, are part of the media environment that surrounds us.
And it is not only the typical objectified woman, which must disappear whatsoever.
I am talking about those cages of stereotypes which in 2018 will no longer exist in the United Kingdom because the Advertising Standards Authority decided to use new guidelines against stereotypes.
Fines will be paid by those advertisers where all the family messes up and only mom cleans up; those where a girl activity is presented as not suitable for boys, and viceversa; those where a man is mocked because he unsuccesfully tries to take care of the house and the family.
Do we want these stereotypes-free ads too?
Then let's unite, men and women,
and speak up together. This involves agencies' creative staff, ad campaigns contractors, decisions-makers in companies and all of us as consumers.
The third recipe is the political one: there is a huge taboo in Italy that we've got to get rid of, the fact that parental leave should be a women's business.
I know, every time we try to give men one more day in fully paid parental leave public finances suffer; but habits don't change by allowing one day at a time.
Habits are changing everywhere: in companies like Facebook, which offers its employees, when they become fathers, up to four months of paternal leave; or Spotify, which offers six months.
But aside of those big and successful companies, which can afford these egualitarian policies, what can we do?
In Italy, we have a big resource: after the compulsory leave, the voluntary parental leave. It's a period of six months now, partly paid, only for women.
It is politically possible, in case of a coalition between men and women, to create a bipartisan volountary leave: three months for a parent and three months for the other, that can't be exchanged or given to someone else.
In this way, women would come back to work earlier, for everyone's great benefit; fathers could learn caring duties and maybe, like Swedish, they could live longer; and third, society as a whole could work better.
In an ideal world, men and women with a newborn would stay at home for the same period.
No one in a company would be denied an assignment, a project, a promotion, a rise in salary because a baby could be born.
We can no longer afford, men and women, not to be feminists.
Building bridges is the only way to recover from the poison in this world.
With the three recipes I explained you today, we could go a long way.
That's why I'm going to leave you with the words of a feminist man.
Thank you.
(Applause) It is so important that we all understand that it's not that man can be feminist, it's that men should be
feminists as well. And I am proud of that. Being a feminist, for me, means recognizing that men and women should be, can be, must be equal.
And secondly, that we still have an awful lot of work to do.
He for She is a U.N. movement that I hope all of you go up and sign up for, of men standing up for women.
Men shutting down some of those negative conversations that we get in locker rooms, and in Bro culture.
We need to know that we are better than that!
How we treat our sisters, our girlfriends, our cousins, our mothers and the world around us, matters!
We need to take back what it needs to be a man and that means being open, compassionate, respectful, and brave about standing up for it!
(Applause)
Your Holiness, I started writing two years ago,
one morning in late springtime in a village in the Langhe district with cobblestone streets that spiral up to the square.
There's a castle and an old church in the square, like those you find all over Piedmont.
From that square, the valley opens up to a breath-taking view. At the end, hills covered with woods, chestnut and hazel trees. Below are vineyards and cornfields.
The beauty of the Langhe! There, the extraordinary beauty of the landscape coexists with the horror of certain families. Like mine, a middle class one, among the indifference of many.
Your Holiness, my name is Ginevra and I’m forty. I’m from Turin, where I grew up and went to high school.
And later on, I met the man I eventually married. It was autumn.
He was from the Langhe district, Cuneo. A slender young man who was so polite.
He had just enrolled to study medicine in Turin, Corso Massimo D’Azeglio.
That day, the fog along the Po river rose up to the park. The atmosphere was chilling, we looked at each other and were happy,
It was a dream that stayed in our blood.
He got his degree, became a specialist. got married and celebrated a lavish wedding.
I moved to his town.
Then came our first son, Matteo: a gift from God.
Then the twins Pietro and Luca.
Pietro – Peter – is the rock on which Jesus founded his church. Luca is light, the Virgin’s favourite; St Luke is the patron of physicians.
Francesca, green eyes and blonde curls, has a kind of shy look. She resembles me.
My children are beautiful!
They have the healthy complexion of countryside people.
At the foot of the Langhe, at the confluence of two rivers, 300 metres above sea level.
It was once a stopping place for wayfarers and pilgrims on the old road linking Albenga to Alba.
We live on a big farm, and during the day the children run barefoot on the lawn and jump for joy; at night the crickets sing.
Andrea is the heir of a wealthy family: they own a building in town and a beautiful home in Sanremo.
His family is well known in our town. They generously donate to the Church and on Sunday he always goes to the service, sitting in the first row, with his family’s name on the pew.
As a young man, he took time off to travel. But instead of marrying a local girl, one accustomed to country life, he chose me.
Turinese, and a “foreigner” in Cuneo. A slim blonde girl like others he met around the world.
Unfortunately, our fairy tale ends here:
my husband doesn't have the virtues of King Arthur. When he got back to his hometown, he changed.
It is probably due to the chauvinistic farming culture. Maybe his friends at the café, for whom women don’t count and are meant to serve them.
Fact is, Andrea got violent. He's been beating me for 12 years, and now he also beats our children.
And no Lancelot comes along to save us. We're not living in some poor suburb. Quite the opposite:
Andrea heads his hospital department.
He has a good salary. He only allows me a few euros at a time, and in the evening he checks back the receipts from the grocery store; he pays the butcher at the end of the month. But all in all, money isn't a problem.
The other night Andrea started kicking me, but I was so tired I couldn’t stand the pain and screamed.
My daughter suddenly woke up and crept onto the stairs, and she saw me on the floor as her father hit me.
The next day, she burst out crying at school. The teacher hugged her and she told her the whole story. She was astonished: she has known Andrea all her life.
She would have never thought he was a violent man.
But children don’t lie.
Her teacher called me in and said she would talk to him.
I begged her not to. That would put me and my daughter at risk of being killed.
As for now, I don’t feel like rebelling. I can’t leave this beautiful house to move into a tiny apartment with my four children.
Plus, my husband is Catholic and doesn’t want a separation.
My parents are also practising Catholics.
They live in the city and I don’t see them often.
My mother has sensed that something is wrong, but I don’t want to load her with my problems. I already know she wouldn’t be happy with a divorced daughter.
I do not want to leave Andrea, actually. I don’t want to go to the police, as my only friend told me, a high school classmate I sometimes meet.
It would be like betraying him.
Maybe I’m doing something wrong: it all seems so strange to me.
When I met Andrea, he was kind - he still is. He beats me, then he hugs me and says he loves me.
It happens all the time.
If I’m bleeding, he naturally takes care of it. No ER, of course: everyone knows him there.
Families often become a place of peaceful cruelty, instead of love.
The other day I got home and his mother was there. In her youth, she beated her husband and children. At home, she doesn’t speak in Italian, just dialect.
In that rough language, a few nights ago she egged Andrea on to beat me.
Then she was the very one who cast me off. My children looked on us, astonished.
Maybe that's because people and cattle are all the same thing here. I’m from Turin and I come from a respectable middle-class family.
She would have preferred a local girl, one of those who stay at home and don’t say a word.
On Sunday I went to confess. Father Paolo said, I need to be patient, to put up with this.
Jesus says that the people who face the greatest suffering are God’s chosen ones.
I must not complain. Family is sacred and cannot be torn apart.
Of course, it’s hard to stay together without mutual respect. But that’s not just about respect. There is violence, and violence kills love.
After beating me, Andrea confesses his sins, and the priest forgives him.
Then it happens again, he beats me again, and Father Paolo forgives him yet again.
He is a country priest, and my mother-in-law brings him chickens and rabbits. Things of the past.
<i>Andrea</i> means man.
Your Holiness, during the service that started your papacy, you said that every man must be the keeper of himself and others.
Andrea was watching the service on TV. Then it took some pretext for him to beat me again, in front of the children. It has become routine, even for me.
I stay silent as I stare at my man, distraught.
I raised four children, but time didn't pass for women, here: they bear children and have no voice.
I am covered in bruises and I hide my scarred cheekbone with a bit of makeup. I have to do something for them, for my children. The oldest has started hitting other people.
Your Holiness, I heard you on the radio, speaking about the first believers. I would like to ask for your help.
Maybe all it takes is a word of yours to end all this violence. I’ve heard it called “violence disguised as love”.
But maybe it would have no effect.
What can you say that hasn’t been said already?
Unless you, Your Holiness, decide to talk to priests like Father Paolo. To not grant forgiveness to men like my husband.
The Church should only offer forgiveness when there's honest repentance. Because formal repentance allows people to access to the sacraments, and then return to the usual violence.
One must show repentance, try to mend oneself.
Before a fake contrition, a priest cannot – and must not – absolve that person.
[Two years later, April 18th 2015] Your Holiness, I wrote you two years ago, but you had just become Pope and it was the wrong time.
You already know me, I'm Ginevra, I'm from Piedmont like your grandfather Giovanni and his father Francesco.
I live in Turin: beautiful and ancient, and when the evening becomes a star there is a huge crowd of people!
Turin is like Naples that goes to the mountains. Turin, with its straight roads, is the flip side of Rome. Turin, a city suffering from melancholy, as Venditti sang.
We live in a condominium on Corso Francia, a straight road that goes from Piazza Statuto, Porta Susa, and leads to Chambéry in France.
It was built by the Savoy ruler Victor Amadeus II in 1711 and connected the Royal Palace to the residence in Rivoli.
During the Second World War, when Italy went to war against France, the city authorities changed its name to Corso Gabriele d’Annunzio.
After the war, it got called back Corso Francia. The underground runs along here every day, with nine stops!
At the beginning of Corso Francia there are lots of lovely Art Nouveau residences built in early 20th century.
We live further down, towards Rivoli, on the outskirts of Turin.
Office workers live in my building
and they leave early to rush to work.
Where I’m living is not my house.
It’s where I grew up, my parents’ house.
They are elderly and left me their furnished apartment, and then moved to the countryside.
Now I’d like to tell you how I worked up the courage to go to the police and sue Andrea.
I didn’t want to at the time.
Francesca was in fourth grade when her father kicked me on the rug.
The next morning she told her teacher about it.
Concerned that the teacher would go talk to Andrea about it
It didn’t look good for my daughter to go around and say what happened.
I begged her not to say anything, as he would have beaten both me and my daughter.
I had taken this tough decision
for Francesca, now a sixth grader.
After talking to the teacher,
I went to the police and to the emergency room,
where they reported my bruises. It wasn’t even the worst beating I went through!
But it was enough to press charges.
A few weeks later I went to a law firm in Turin. I worked with one lawyer for the criminal case and another for the civil suit.
No legal aid from the State: I needed someone I could count on.
I spent everything I had on lawyers.
It was a huge sacrifice.
Now Andrea gives me 500 euros a month per child.
Wealthy as he is, he should have given me more. but properties are all in his mother’s name.
He doesn’t care if our children no longer have the same chances.
He doesn’t care about my efforts to pay for English and violin lessons.
The children’s life has changed. They give up a lot of things, they go to public school. Yet they don’t complain,
and they’ve never asked to go back to their father's town.
They don’t miss that old farmhouse with frescoed vaulted ceilings, the terrace, those beautiful arches.
I work part-time as a secretary at a museum, for 900 euros a month.
We have a lot of expenses, although I pay my parents just 500 euros a month in rent. A symbolic figure. They're both retired teachers with a few health problems. They get by, but I can’t ask for more.
They already do a lot and are not wealthy.
They are practising Catholics, and after many reservations they have finally accepted the fact that I left my husband and asked for a separation.
Can you imagine, two years ago they asked me to bear it, to turn the other cheek.
However, my siblings don't accept my decision.
They live outside Turin, and I only see them occasionally.
I know I’m running out of time now, Your Holiness. Let me just tell you about how Andrea and his family reacted. When I left, he was so upset.
Now he’s acting like a country gentleman hurt in his honour, and I’m painted as the crazy one.
During the week he's in his white coat at the emergency room. On Saturdays he volunteers at the Green Cross and works with the elderly every so often.
Service every Sunday, of course.
I’m the one who made it all up.
He has filed against me
in court: five lawsuits.
My mother-in-law took it worse, now a sort of wild animal with a wounded pride. She thought silence would protect everyone.
She thinks I’m a witch to send to the asylum. She doesn’t understand why I would stand up for myself, get rid of my country lady life to live in a suburban apartment that needs a reprise.
I’ve only whitewashed it, she kept everything.
I only have a few clothes. No jewellery at all, he never gave me any.
My string of pearls?
My mother's gift for my 18th birthday, it’s a tradition in Piedmont.
I’ve never gone back to that town, it’s Andrea’s territory.
I can’t go back to that house, not even to get my things. I ran away from there. Two years ago it was June, and schools were about to end. I barely picked up my children’s report cards.
One evening Andrea had the night shift at the hospital.
I took the children and left, heading to Turin. We drove 80 kilometres in an old car that Andrea's lawyers got back a few months later.
We left no friends behind in the village, neither me nor my children. We were isolated, because their father never wanted anyone over.
It's not been easy, but I did it. <i>We</i> did it.
Your Holiness, I am not asking you for anything. I just wanted to tell you what happened.
I go to to Church every Sunday,
and I take my children with me. They were accustomed to that beautiful old church. After the service, as the bells were chiming, we would go into the square, enjoying a view of the Langhe.
Now we go to the neighbourhood parish, in Corso Francia.
It’s not the same thing, but that’s fine.
I spoke to a priest about my story, one I happened to meet on a train.
He made an impression on me. His name is Father Carlo Caroglio, and he said we must not submit to violence. He is a city priest, modern. A native of Alessandria, he had lived in Novara for many years. Before becoming a priest, he trained as a chemical technologist. Just like you.
Father Carlo was different from the town priest, the one who keeps telling Andrea: “It’s not your fault if your wife left you. A wife who leaves you doesn’t love you. She’s the one who should feel ashamed. She’s not worthy of you.”
Andrea would beat me, then he would go to the priest, said he was so sorry, and the priest would absolve him.
That’s why Andrea now feels he’s right.
Your Holiness, I have nothing more to say – I don’t have time now.
I trust in your goodness and understanding.
I have just one thing to ask: please, do not allow priests to absolve violent men no matter what.
My warmest regards, Ginevra.
(Applause)
Let me tell you a story.
A few weeks ago, I brought my daughter Ilaria, 10 years old, to school.
But I wasn't dressed like this, I had no business suit.
My daughter, curious as the 10-year-old girl she is, said to me, "Dad, you don't go to work today."
I replied, "No, today I am working from home, actually."
She looked at me and said, "Well, you probably don't have anything relevant to do."
(Laughter) And this thing made me think a bit.
It made me think how much we actually nurture prejudices in our children.
I'm going to tell you that in reality, according to my point of view, work is not a place one goes to.
Work is undergoing a profound change.
I will tell you some things that support this thesis.
Some skills are becoming increasingly rare, but more and more required by market and companies.
A BCG study has certified that about 30% of high-tech positions will be vacant in the next 18-24 months, out of skill shortage.
Imagine that: 30% of jobs will remain uncovered due to lack of skills, especially in the world of data science and machine learning, the most modern, advanced skills.
So, from this point of view, we are in a bit of a trouble to build the skills we want to see in our organisations of the future.
We chase after them.
Another interesting fact is that about 66% of CIOs, those who are responsible for companies' IT infrastructures, say that talent scarcity is becoming a crisis, so it is becoming a very strong theme.
Another very important element: artificial intelligence and automation and robotisation are changing the world of work.
Always the same BCG study - which I invite you to read, it's called '12 Forces That Will Radically Change How Organizations Work' - has certified that over the next 10 years, 70% of the professions we have today, will have changed drastically or even disappeared.
And if we look at what the World Economic Forum says, two-thirds of the children who are now studying at primary school are preparing for jobs that don’t exist yet.
Impressive, if I think about my children who are attending primary school today.
Another theme: the average life of some skills, of some engineering skills, has dropped dramatically and is today between two and a half and five years.
What does this mean?
That if a person does not make up their mind to invest in themselves and engage in continuous learning, they will soon be fairly unemployable from the job market.
But there are also some slightly softer forces that are changing the world of work.
For example, having a strong corporate culture.
The fact that about 90% of companies consider culture a distinctive and competitive factor for the success of the organization is certainly something very significant, something very important.
And, from this point of view, there is correspondence,
because even the people living in organizations are putting culture and sense of purpose at the centre of their path.
62% of Millennials don't want a job, they don't want a salary, they don't want an office job.
They want a career with a strong social impact.
And in my opinion, probably it's not just the Millennials that want a job, a career that helps them to have a strong social impact.
Another soft force that is changing the world of work is the growing attention to the outer world, the theme of diversity and inclusion.
In the United States, 80% of the people employed in organisations declare that a key factor, in choosing their position and their company. is how much diversity those companies are willing to embrace.
And 72% of the same people say they are willing to leave the organisation they work for for another that is more inclusive and pays more attention to diversity.
Thus, it is becoming an element of attraction of talents.
It is becoming such a strong theme that McKinsey has done a study that has shown that there is a 15% more chance, for companies that seriously invest on the topic of diversity and inclusion, to achieve financial success.
So, it's not just something cute, something nice to work with.
It is a differential factor for the economic results of the organisation.
Finally, work - and this is probably my main point - is increasingly fluid and flexible.
There is the explosion of the co-working spaces.
In Italy we have some beautiful success stories on this subject.
Smart working has become a theme at the heart of corporate strategies.
The smart working observatory of the Politecnico di Milano claims that by now most of the Milan companies have adopted strategies for smart working.
And then there is another equally important theme: skills are being formed in places where, until a few years ago, it was unthinkable to see them.
Let's think about how many graduates there are in software engineering in India or China.
And this is forcing organizations to rethink their strategies.
If until yesterday the organisations put an office where it made sense to put it and then attracted the talent, today the same organisations are questioning this approach, they are wondering if this strategy is really the right one.
And they are putting the seats where talent already is.
The explosion of jobs, especially with technological content, in India is very strong in recent years.
Work is always more fluid and flexible; it is increasingly normal, commonplace to see a team where the boss is based in Washington and the five members of the staff are based in Caracas, Madrid, Greece, Shanghai or Sydney, maybe across five or six different timezones.
So, from this point of view, do we still need an office?
Well, hardly so I think.
I doubt it because, as I told you, in a world of work that is changing very strongly, where skills are increasingly scarce, the war for talent is always stronger, where artificial intelligence, automation are changing the way companies work, where purpose, diversity, inclusion positively put themselves at the center of corporate strategies, in a world where organisations are evidently increasingly fluid and flexible, I think that no, it no longer makes much sense.
And so, here's my simple, clear idea: to help our children overcome prejudices, to help them question them
and dismiss, if they have any.
Because the world of work is changing, the world of work will be marked by an activity that is done with more and more passion, with more and more perceived meaning, with more and more desire to focus on the theme of continuous learning I have tried to convey to you.
And so, our purpose is this: to talk to our children about this topic, to talk about how, from a practical point of view, work cannot be a physical space, it cannot be an office.
We don't "go to work," even if we say it often.
We do work.
And so, my point is exactly this: work must necessarily be, in our narration to our children,
something that is done, certainly not a place to go to.
Thank you.
(Applause)
Good afternoon, everybody.
Today I'm going to bring you to my world.
I will speak about diversity in the blockchain sector,
but let’s first take a step back; what is the blockchain?
Blockchain is a new layer of the Internet with some fundamental characteristics: it is open, immutable, incorruptible, transparent, and decentralized.
And it is commonly called the Web 3.0.
If Web 1.0 was the era where companies communicated directly with customers, in a two-way fashion: companies were promoting their products on the Internet; and users bought them from an e-commerce platform. The era of Web 2.0, instead, was the era when users started to interact each other, sharing information and data on blogs, social media, and so on. The blockchain enables us to trade value without intermediaries.
We know that this technology will impact many industries, not only finance, and we know we need to be prepared for that.
We're not sure when it will happen, but we know that not only we need to be prepared in terms of products but also in terms of training the workforce that will develop this technology.
In my company, for example, we believe in the so-called "tokenization of everything"
In other words, we think that every asset, every form of value, one day - be it houses, cars, precious metals, luxury, artistic goods, will be digitalized in a token so that it can be traded on this new blockchain technology.
Just to give you a few examples, imagine the textile and food industries, where blockchain could help track the supply chain, for example against forgery. Or imagine art freaks, who cannot afford a whole masterpiece, let's say by Andy Warhol, but can invest in a fraction of this: and they can trade this token, with other fractions of art items, again, as an investment.
But if we know that blockchain technology will impact on everyone's life, through so many products, the workforce behind it needs to reflect this openness, must be able to speak to everyone.
Today I will talk to you about diversity, the issue I most advocate for in this sector.
I have looked for a good definition of it, I wanted to come here with a single, compelling one, but I actually found so many different ones, as diversity and inclusion can be declined to several contexts:
sexual orientation, ethnicity, social-economic background.
In my opinion, the best definition of diversity in business is the ability to build a workforce where anyone feels accepted, no matter who they are, feel like they are contributing to the future of the company.
We intuitively know that diversity matters, but now we also have data-driven studies showing that companies with the highest diversity through all the management layer, also have the highest financial returns.
This suggests it's not just about gender diversity, but also all the other aforementioned types of diversity.
But, how come?
Why is diversity a driver for success in business?
Because companies with more diversity develop higher sensibility in understanding what the market needs, what customers want.
But where are we now? It's a time for diversity in tech, including, but not limited to blockchain.
At Circle, my company, we actually wanted to evaluate the interest of women for blockchain and more specifically for cryptos, our field of interest.
So we ran a survey in the US, one of our market, on more than 3,000 people ranging from 24 to 74 years old.
Guess what?
Cryptocurrencies and blockchain appeal to millennial, white, men.
That's the status quo.
And the results of this study, compared with other studies and surveys, proved to be robust; there's a five percent of women, think about it, only five percent, who belong to the workforce of this field, started blockchain companies, invest in it, are passionate, engineers.
My bottom line today is: to work in the blockchain sector, there is no need to be a 20-year-old nerd, who spend all day in front of a screen, have a degree in engineering, and are passionate about finance and investments.
Think about me, for instance: I want to briefly share my background with you, because I don’t think it's a coincidence that I joined this new innovative sector. I come from the sharing economy,
a totally different world.
I have no STEM background: BA, with specialization in marketing.
I traveled the world, for both school and business reasons. and since I was a baby I've always been an early adopter,
I have always been the first to try something new, eager to discover that innovation that would succeed later on.
And I've been lucky, because of my first example - I grew up alone with my mother.
So my family was made of me, my mother, and my dog.
and she always - instead of tying me, keeping me, she always pushed me to go and try.
And that's what I did. I grew up surrounded by the Piedmont's fog until I was 19 years old. Then I moved to Milan to study.
After three years in Milan, that was my dream ever since, it seemed unreachable to me, and it was kind of over. I felt it was time for me to move on.
So I lived in New York, London, Paris, both for study and business.
Think about it: when I approached business life for the first time, the tech startup wave was just starting in Europe.
And I immediately realized, I wanted to belong there.
Even if it was much more risky than working for established corporations, I wanted to be there,
elaborating new business models that would impact on millions of people's lives before they even knew.
And so I crossed the path of a then small California-based startup who had started hiring to open the European market: it was a 100 people company, at that moment. I sent my resume,
after just two hours they replied,
and after a week they hired me.
So together with two other people, I launched Uber's Italian market.
I worked in Uber for four years, and I have to tell you, it was such a fascinating experience.
After four years in the Italian market, I said, "It's time for another challenge.
Move me to India." I want to experience an emerging market on a completely different scale. I want to see what happens there and how this technology can be adapted.
I lived in Mumbai for a few months and I start hearing people talking about the blockchain, a technology I sincerely wasn't aware of before.
I get more and more interested.
And so I realized: okay, here we go again.
Now that the model of sharing economy was already successful and proven, that is impacting in all the world's areas, I look for this innovation, this early-stage technology no one still believes in.
So I start looking for companies that are focused on blockchain - until I stumble in my current one.
Initially, I had many doubts: I'll for sure be surrounded by male tech geeks who spend their days in front of a computer.
And I will not belong there.
I went through the recruiting process, during which I fell in love with my company and my colleagues.
First, I met my manager, which is a hero for me. And then several inspiring women with leading roles, from the European Director for Communication to the Legal Director of Europe.
So these two factors: the recruitment process, where I totally feel welcomed, and I become sure a space was there for me, and the innovation factor - I wanted to be among the first ones to find and try that thing - broke my last resistances to entering this world.
But let's take a step back. the world is composed of 50 percent of women and 50 percent of men.
And what is technology for, at the end of the day?
Solving everyone's everyday problems.
Therefore, in our companies, even the workforce behind these products should mirror this gender diversity.
I believe that companies - but not only them, institutions also play a key role.
Let’s start from the institutions: classes of computer science are held for children, and later on MBAs, master courses, contests in classic computer science. The same should be true for blockchain and cryptocurrencies.
I want to tell you a funny anecdote, one that, guess what, took place a few nights ago, while I was writing this speech, so I said to myself: "I must share this."
There was a TV show on the background, “La TV delle ragazze” [Girls' TV] with Amalia Ercole Finzi. Turned out, she was the first Italian women graduated in aerospace engineering and she told the audience a simple, interesting thing:
Young girls should be given both an Erector Set and a doll.
Not because all little girls will become engineers, that won't happen.
But, in case even just one of them wants to, she should know she's entitled to it.
To tell you about this episode, I was wondering what is the female counterpart term for the word "engineer" in Italian.
And I discovered, some Italian words just have no female counterpart: words like "surgeon", for instance, or "engineer." The word "Major"!
That's the role of the Institutions, for example regarding education: introduce dedicated classes and give little girls the chance to discover this world, get in early touch with it.
And what about companies?
For sure, they should expose their female leadership: let's show these women,
let's interview them, let's expose them to the media.
Behind these thriving companies, the world should know, are young women who traveled all around the world, with a stellar resume.
On the other hand, of course, recruiting should be reconsidered.
There is a very interesting study done by Hewlett-Packard.
According to this study, women will only apply for a job if they feel 100% qualified. The men's threshold to consider the same application is just 60%.
Imagine the consequences of that in my field, FinTech - we combine finance and technology, historically two male sectors.
It's so hard to attract valid female candidates, women who could have a great career, to be determinant in writing the white canvas for this technology.
And the approach to resume should also change.
A resume is no longer the foundation of the recruiting process: A headhunter's job is now to detect what's the talent, the appetite of this person, looking at their soft skill rather than their hard skill.
Maybe their resume has nothing to do with blockchain or cryptocurrencies: take me, I had nothing to do with them.
Or maybe, they already invest in cryptocurrencies, in their free time. They already made their research, maybe, out of personal passion, interest.
So that's what we, as companies, should set our mind to.
And what about individuals?
What's our power today?
Well, I have taken you into my world, sharing my gender-diverse reality.
I have had five bosses so far, in my career: three women and two men, one of whom was black and gay.
They're an endless source of inspiration for me: I'd die to be like them.
What you can do, instead, from tomorrow on is start getting informed: not necessarily apply for a company, but learning something more about this new technology.
Maybe you develop an interest and start playing an active role in this revolution, instead of just taking it passively once it will impact on everyone's lives.
Because it will have an impact, and it will be greater and deeper than the Internet had over the last 20 years.
So I encourage you all - should I give a suggestion to a fresh college graduate, but also to you, it would be: bring your backpack, put a T-shirt inside and go to Asia, where this field is booming,
Go to meetups, cocktails, events,
start getting what it's all about.
Do like this little girl behind me, who is having mindless fun trying this new mean she doesn't fully know, following her attitude,
and enjoying the ride.
I encourage all of you to do the same.
Do it, right now.
Thank you.
(Applause)
This is Juan, he is a native Kaqchikel, and he lives in Guatemala.
He is a Mayan, Kaqchikel native.
Like the majority of the Kaqchikel, Juan grows corn.
The Maya believe they are made of corn.
Their god Huracán, in the Popul Vuh, the Mayan sacred text, moulds them out of corn paste.
And so, every year, before the dry season, Juan sows corn.
Each year, he needs 500 dollars to buy seeds, 600 dollars to buy pesticides to protect those seeds and 900 dollars to buy fertilizers to grow those seeds.
It's called the "full technological pack"; that is how it is sold.
A technological pack including seeds, pesticides and fertilizers, that costs 2,000 dollars.
Needless to say, Juan doesn't have 2,000 dollars. he needs to go to the bank, to borrow that money.
And by the time he finally delivers, sells his corn, and pays back his debt, the amount he owes will be 2,400 dollars.
The problem is that when he sells his corn in Guatemala --between Guatemala and the U.S. there is a free trade agreement, and the goods produced in Guatemala and in the U.S. circulate freely between the two Countries.
And so, when Juan goes to sell his corn, he has to compete with the corn produced in the U.S., where they grow it like this.
Thanks to an ultra-mechanized, industrialized agricolture, and to large-scale cultivation, corn in the U.S. is produced at an infinitely lower price than Juan's corn.
The price of the corn has dropped so much, that now Juan sells it at 10 dollars per 100 kilograms. 10 cents per kilogram.
But the year was good, and Juan managed to produce 500 kilograms of corn,
that will earn him 5,000 dollars. And when we subtract the 2,400 dollars that Juan needs to return to the bank, that leaves 2,600 dollars for the entire harvest.
This means 220 dollars a month.
But Juan keeps some of that corn for himself, and he also has a small garden and a few hens.
Thanks to all of this he is able to provide for himself, his wife and his three kids 1,700 kcal a day.
These 1,700 kcal a day, that for us might sound like the perfect low-calorie diet that helps us flatten our stomach for the summer, for Juan, they mean a loss of 1 kg a month, every month.
12 kg a year, every year.
This is called malnutrition.
In Guatemala, 22 families own 76% of the land.
9,5 million farmers share what is left.
The plots of land are so small that they cannot produce much, so that Guatemala is the tenth most malnourished Country in the world.
2,244 kcal are the average in Guatemala.
The average.
And this includes people in the cities, wasted food, the rich plump people who live in the cities, and it includes millions of farmers whose intake won't reach those crucial 2,000 calories, and never will.
According to the World Bank, 49.8% of Guatemalan children under the age of five are malnourished.
According to the same study of the World Bank, last year, 72% of Guatemalan families had to sell some of their assets in order to be able to buy food.
Asset, this word used by the World Bank that, to us, might sound like a high finance term, for a farmer means a hoe, a shovel, a plough.
It means selling work tools in order to buy food.
And so, what does Juan do?
He sees a future, something different.
He sees billboards advertising a crop that could guarantee him a higher income. It's the sugar cane.
A U.S. firm produces biodiesel in Guatemala.
They would lend him 3,000 dollars, and his debt would grow.
But that same firm promises Juan to buy his corn for 6,000 dollars at the end of the year.
The profit, for Juan, grows from 220 to 250 dollars per month.
Sounds like the best deal of the year.
Juan, gives it a try.
He goes for the sugar cane.
However, now, Juan has no corn at the end of the year. and what seemed to be a perfect business turns out to be a flop.
And so, his calories go from 1,700 to 1,600.
He won't be losing 12 kg a year now, but 15.
He can't make it, it's impossible.
And so, what does he do?
He decides to sell his plot of land to the very firm that will produce sugar cane on a large scale, and will turn it into biodiesel.
He sells his plot of land for about 7,000 dollars.
It's a good piece of land, for Guatemala, on a fertile, flat terrain.
Juan even sells his home and he earns about 6,000 dollars.
And then he makes another debt, for 10,000 dollars.
And with these 23,000 dollars Juan will pay a coyote, a trafficker of human beings, who will take him away and will help him cross Mexico, on this train, a train that in Latin America is known as "The Beast". Perched on this train, every single day, thousands of migrants travel
to escape, to flee from their countries to reach the United States.
For a month, Juan will travel in brutal conditions.
Perched, at times tied, for the fear of falling if he dozes off.
He is tied to this silo, transporting that very biodiesel produced from the crops he grew.
Juan paid 23,000 dollars to be illegally transported from Guatemala towards the U.S.
And those 500,000 liters of biodiesel are being transported at the cost of 350 dollars.
Why am I telling you all of this?
And why me?
Well, because I lived for a long time in Latin America, in Guatemala,
and I have heard hundreds of stories like Juan's.
So, one day, I said to myself that someone had to tell the story and had to come here to tell what happens.
In order to do so, first I had to deprive myself of something and get rid of it, I had to get rid of this shield that we all have, this protection we receive at birth, the most precious thing we have.
You might think, when one is born, to a newborn, we usually say, "It's your health that counts, as long as you are in good health, and that you have enough money, and the sun is shining, and you live somewhere beautiful, and your family loves you."
There's something much more important, something you are assigned, randomly, at birth.
Something we cannot touch, intangible, that we cannot see, but that we all have.
And it is one of the most violent things ever invented: our citizenship.
Citizenship is represented by this dumb brownish booklet that we all have.
They give you one when you're born, almost like winning the lottery.
I won it, because mine says "Repubblica italiana".
With this, I can access the best educational systems, and the best health care.
With this I can enter 173 Countries without a visa.
There are people, just like me, whose passport, however, says "Republic of Syria", and with that, they won't be going anywhere.
In 1853, the United States needed to build the Trans Pacific railway, the one that runs from the Atlantic to the Pacific.
But they found an obstacle: the central area of the United States, in Arizona, it was all mountains.
And so they had no way to build the railway. It was complicated, it was a mess.
So, what did they do?
They contacted Mexico, that had zero money, like today, and bought a piece of land from it.
The sale of the Mesilla.
A few men, in Washington, drew a straight line, like this one, in the middle of the desert, because they needed the plain to build their railway.
Today, the border runs along that straight line.
And there is a town in the middle of that straight line, its name is Nogales.
Nogales is cut into two parts: if you are born on that side you get a U.S. passport.
You can access the best schools
and the best health care.
You have a passport that allows you to enter 167 Countries in the world.
If you are born on this side, you are Mexican, and, with your passport, you can't do anything.
You thought that health mattered most?
Your passport is what counts.
And it might be assigned to you because of a straight line drawn in Washington 160 years ago.
But I wanted to tell the story, and experience it like they do.
But to do so, I had to get rid of this passport.
So, I put it in an envelope, and I mailed it to a friend's house.
I camouflaged, I transformed myself into a Peruvian migrant from Amazonia, by the name of Aymar Blanco.
I crossed the Suchiate river, that separates Mexico from Guatemala, and then I crossed the entire country of Mexico.
Since 1994 Mexico has been collaborating with the Unites States to block illegal immigration.
Each year, the U.S. pours between 4 and 6 billion dollars into Mexico, so that Mexico can do its dirty work and stop Central and South American migrants.
So, now, the border is not just the line between Mexico and the U.S.
The border is the entire Mexican territory!
And Mexico is endless, it so long, and huge.
Depending on where you cross it, it can be more than 2,500 miles.
For the most part of the journey you travel perched, wedged in, at times tied, like Juan, on these freight trains,
known all over the Americas as "The Beast", or the train of death.
Father Solalinde called these railroad tracks the "largest open-air cemetery in the world."
Every year, thousands of people fall asleep, or get distracted. They fall off the train, getting sucked up under the tracks.
This train is the emblem of our modern world; a moving train, an endless cargo train that won't carry passengers, but runs non-stop carrying low-cost goods from the South to the North, and yet, it will not carry passengers.
Coal, iron, zinc, copper, pieces of machinery, scraps of clothing, finished clothing, mangos, papayas, bananas.
Everything travels freely without taxes, without borders, without anything.
The only things that, on that train, are travelling illegally, are human beings.
During my journey I was abducted three times.
A gang of hooded men, in the middle of the desert, after robbing us of everything emptied our water supplies.
It was the only thing left to do, so they emptied our bottles right in our faces, so that we could go thirsty.
I had to run, flee, jump off a moving train several times, run faster than pregnant women and elderly men to be able to get away.
I had to plug my ears, close my eyes, not to hear the screams of those who didn't make it.
Amnesty International reports that 60% of women, before entering the U.S., get raped during their journey.
60%.
The Federal Police caught us and arrested us. They locked us into a 52 square foot cell, where there were 40 of us.
For two days, at 104 degrees in the day, with no water, no food, no bathroom.
40 people: we had to take turns to sit.
Not even to lie down, just to sit.
There was no way to sit at the same time. We took turns, alternating, to sit, and stand.
For two days straight.
We were forced to eat, drink, shit, piss, vomit, faint, all together in that space.
At some point there was no train, the rain had washed it off the tracks, so they replaced it with a truck service.
And we spent 26 hours hidden in the false bottom of a truck.
At the end of the leg, our joints were so stiff that we fell face down in the mud.
Our drivers were laughing and drinking tequila.
Despite all of this, thousands of people, forced by desperation, begin this journey and reach the U.S. border, and I did it with them.
The U.S. border can virtually be divided into three areas.
You arrive, after crossing Mexico in its entirety, but you still have to cross the border, you still have to make it into that "promised land."
The most eastern part is the only one with an actual natural border, the Río Bravo, a river with a funny name, because in the U.S. they call it the Río Grande del Sur, the big southern river.
In Mexico, they call it the Río Bravo, the evil river.
Hundreds, possibly thousand of people drown, each year, in their attempt to cross it.
The current drags them out to sea, where the Gulf of Mexicos currents, as a last insult, take them into Texas Dead.
The 7 counties of the Southern Texan Coast have been in a state of medical emergency since 2008, due to the number of dead bodies washing onto their coast every day.
Go West, and you have the wall: 760 miles, the second largest separation barrier ever built by human beings.
In 20 years, that wall has already seen the death - according to the U.S. official numbers - of 3,040 people.
So that you can compare, during the 28 years of the Berlin wall, the number of deaths amounted to 98.
Officially, we talk of 3,040.
In Mexico, the estimate is around 15,000.
And then, in the middle, there is nothing, a gap.
What else could you add in addition to the Sonora desert, a desert as large as a third of Italy, with temperatures exceeding 104 during the day, and plummetting to 14 degrees at night.
Pima county, just over the border, declared bankruptcy in 2013.
They could not pay the power bills to run the freezers where they stored 871 unidentified corpses of migrants who had died in their attempt to cross the desert.
And that is where I crossed.
You walk at night, it's freezing cold and hide during the day in the scorching heat.
Groups of "U.S. patriots" poison the water wells. One night we found a poisoned well. I got sick.
I owe my life to a Nicaraguan migrant, whose name I don't even know.
While I was vomiting behind a cactus he grabbed my shoulder and said, "Hey, look, they are leaving you here.
Move it!"
Not everyone has a Nicaraguan guardian angel.
We left in a group of 24 and only 19 made it to Tucson.
Today, through that desert, they want to add to, build yet another wall, a further barrier to separate the two countries, a further barrier to prevent human beings to do the most natural thing ever: move, relocate.
3.5 million years ago we were monkeys,
and we climbed down the trees to move.
We acquired the upright position, as bipeds, to be able to walk long distances, to be able to move.
Human history is made of countless relocations, countless migrations and transfers from one place to the next, in search of food, in search of partners.
And then we go back, we flee from catastrophes.
Human beings move.
Infants learn to walk before they learn to properly use their hands.
Infants walk before they learn to speak.
They learn to walk before eating solid foods.
The human being is made for walking.
And no wall, no barrier in the world, for as large as they might build it, no barrier in the world will ever prevent human beings from moving from place to place.
Thank you.
(Applause) [Roots are important in the life of a person, but we, as humans, have legs, not roots, and legs are made to go elsewhere - Pino Cacucci]
Today we are talking about future, youth, technology, but also tradition, know-how, art and beauty.
Today I'll take you to a factory and tell you about the incredible opportunity that lies at the border between mechanics and digital.
It was the early 80s, and I still remember my Saturday mornings with my dad in the workshop.
It was a mechanical workshop, or rather a metal-mechanical one,
and they used to build complicated details for the textile and areonautics industry.
As we walked through the workshop I remember a dark environment, an acrid smell of oil all around me, my dad running everywhere giving out instructions to whoever crossed his path.
I remember busy men with big and strong hands, their heads inside the machines, inside their dark outfit.
I also remember great order and cleanliness - my father was so keen on tidiness.
I remember really precise mechanical details; they looked like jewels, artworks: the know-how.
Let’s now take a step forward: it’s 2017 and we are living in the so called Fourth Industrial Revolution, or Industry 4.0 -
-- a historical time that is marked by blurrier and much wider borders than in the previous ones.
Let’s track them back together.
The first one, in the 18th century, introduced the use of steam to power factory equipment.
The second one, in the early 20th century, saw electricity, chemistry, petroleum, mass production and assembly lines - Ford, in a word.
The third one, in the early 1970s, brought along electronics, computers, the first robots and factory automation.
This fourth one is instead substantially marked by the connection between physical and digital systems.
And it differs from the previous ones in at least five major ways.
The first one is speed; the first industrial revolution reached a tipping point in 80 years, the second one made it in 50 years, the third 30, and the fourth is hitting us like a tsunami.
Multidimensionality and width, which refers not only to single technological dimensions but to many of them in acceleration and in combination with each other;
it refers not only to a factory's internal processes, but also, increasingly, to products, commodity chains, business models -- it’s all interconnected.
Democratization: the cost of access to these technologies is dropping dramatically, it is no longer reserved solely to big companies but to an ever-growing public.
Human capital is at the very center of this fourth revolution, much more than in the first three.
It is a cultural revolution, before a technological one.
Like any other field, mechanics is also affected by this big challenge and big transformations.
Despite a world-wide crisis, mechanics remain a cornerstone of the Italian industry, which remains Europe's second biggest manufacturer, fifth in the world for added value.
Mechanics alone accounts for 48% of Italian exports and 82% of high and mid-high technology production.
We, people in the field, are at the core of this fourth transformation. We witness the convergence between mechanics, digital and electronics,
not only in the processes, but more and more into the end products.
We call the product of this convergence "mechatronics".
This convergence will lead to 26 billion interconnected objects by 2020.
Smarter and smarter products, meant to make our life easier and safer, that keep on learning, once in the market, collect a tremendous wealth of data, feel, react.
How will industries adapt themselves to this new context?
In our company, we make products to move and meter fluids with great precision and accuracy, and given this growing complexity, a few years ago we started asking ourselves just that.
In 2013 we started a completely detached place, a separated company, a knowledge-based hub where designers and engineers work together with an eye on the future.
It's a group of people who put themselves at the center of a network of excellence made up of universities, research centers, experts, and tech partners.
They all work with an open mode, acting and working as architects of innovation, combining technology, people, knowledge.
This product is a prominent example of this work.
And it's a valve, a device to deviate a flow, to be used in the medical field. The extraordinary thing about it is that it has been not only entirely projected but also tested by a computer through hundreds and hundreds of reiterated simulations, whose task was to identify and examine the highest possible number of configurations and geometries in order to identify the best one according to a target function.
The result was then rendered through a 3D printer, combined with an actuator made of a shape memory alloy, a smart material which acts both as sensor and actuator, so basically as an engine.
All this makes up a connected product, part of those 26 billion objects which are able to feel, learn, and react according to predefined rules.
These objects are then produced in connected and modern factories where the staff is hyper-qualified, without gender gaps -- no longer. They project and govern complex machines that realize the same ultraprecise and minute mechanical details I remember as a kid - but now, they're mechatronic.
While all this is happening, however, while we ride this wave with great dedication and passion, let me take a step back in time: it was a Sunday morning in 2015, I was home with my kids, back then 11, 9 and 6, talking once again about dad’s work, the factory, mechanics, this big transformation.
It already happened, but that time I felt particularly sorry not to see in their eyes the same excitement, and I started to wonder why that was.
This fascinating transformation we are witnessing every day, this digital convergence, to our kids, families, but also to the general public looks distant, unfamiliar.
There is a dramatic gap between reality and perception,
Sure, we in the industry have never been known for our storytelling abilities. Traditionally we prefer making products to telling stories.
Before my kids’ dull faces, I realized we were missing so much in just sitting down while this gap was increasing.
Industry is where the greatest advancements of our time are taking place,
and we must be able to share it with our kids.
In a new way, with new words.
A sense of social responsibility rose upon me, initially towards my own kids, then towards our youth, our industry, our country, because I realized there was so much at stake.
An engineer as I am, I therefore decided to test this feeling of mine against data.
I got the association of manufacturers involved and we ran an analysis which showed us clearly that the word "metal-mechanics", our field is still labeled with, is perceived as coupled with words like iron, blue overalls, mass production, assembly lines, dirt, fatigue, oil.
It felt like an image straight out of Charlie Chaplin’s “Modern Times”,
a film from the 1930s.
Those companies and the people who bet on the future, the leaders of this paradigm shift, are in no way associated with those words, rather to: technology, precision, electronics, digital, mechanics, future.
Words which are in turn all associated with the term “mechatronics”.
That was a starting point for a project of sector repositioning, in order to make it the first best for our kids,
thus promoting a cultural exchange, and completely rethinking our own narrative.
And so it began with a simple relabeling, from old fashioned "metal-mechanics" to mechatronics,
Then we created an image, a new identity which would communicate simply, and communicate with everyone.
We then went to schools where we involved young people with the “Company Director” contest to get them to tell from their perspective, and through cinema, this brand new world of hi-tech factories;
We got the “Pubblicità Progresso” foundation involved to go on telling this new social change in work and factories.
And then we went to the theater, the Strehler Theater in Milan where we talked about this shift in a piece called “The Importance of Being Mechatronics”. There we talked about young people, the future, but also about art and about beauty,
because the products we make are beautiful as they are projected in a way where beauty and quality are deeply intertwined.
We then took this project and are currently taking it on a national scale, as it is obviously not just a Milan or Lombardy issue.
But let’s go back to our youth, as it's been said that around 65% of kids who enter elementary school today will work jobs that don't exist yet,
and if we want that future to be sustainable, we must get them involved, inspire them, bring them in and welcome them.
But today we set off in Italy from a challenging situation. 65% of STEM skills-based positions go unfilled;
on the other hand, we are often reminded of a 35% of youth unemployment.
It's a paradox, we are talking about two worlds that don’t talk to each other.
But why do our youth and our families make life choices without any prospects?
Are they aware of it?
Or do they choose and make mistakes deliberately because they see this new world as distant, difficult, complex, and so they'd rather take seemingly easier choices?
Robots aren't stealing our jobs, an inadequate education is:
On one hand, the school model has hardly adjusted to our times,
where multidisciplinarity and the ability to handle complex situations is critical, as well as creativity and problem-solving skills --
but we see very little of this in our schools.
Schools should introduce, and let us tinker with these new technologies that are most relevant now. I am talking about blockchain, coding, Internet of Things, Big Data, robotics, smart materials, artificial intelligence --
and I could go on.
All this combined, of course with Art, Literature, and Math.
Schools should be turned into labs of the future,
a diving board towards the future.
Also, education should be felt and lived more and more as an individual responsibility, both for young and old people, because today we have the tools and we can't always wait for someone else to solve all our problems for us.
And us, entrepreneurs, must carry the responsibility of our roles. We must have the ability to spread the vision, the stakes, the soul of this work.
We must leave the factories and go right into the school.
I do it whenever I get a chance to, and believe me, I leave home with the aim of inspiring kids and get back inspired, because they are open and ready, so it's up to us.
We must do this even as parents, as informed, curious, aware parents.
And hadn’t I convinced you yet, come and visit our factories.
Come with your kids,
And don't just come to the factories, but also to all those places where the future is being shaped. I'm talking about incubators, business accelerators,
university labs, research centers.
Italy seems to have lost its vision today, our youth does not take many risks, they seem to have surrendered.
But I don't buy that.
Italy's industrial roots are so deep,
we host a fertile ecosystem filled with extraordinary brains, of passion, tradition of creativity,
and a talent for combinatorial innovation that is envied worldwide.
Let’s not lose all that by letting our kids get lost following futureless paths.
As Nelson Mandela once said: “A winner is just a dreamer who never gives up”.
And if we really want to make our kids succeed again, we must first go back to inspire them and make them dream.
(Applause)
Some of the most attentive members of the audience might guess I come from this region...
We keep a low profile!
I am a healthy carrier of optimism and I like challenges, so when I was asked to choose where I wanted to talk about optimism – if in Silicon Valley or Rovigo, Polesine, Italy I had no doubts because it might be provocative to talk about optimism at this time
Actually, what we believe this time to be is very important and also the idea we have for our future is very important.
Why does the future have much to do with it?
The future has to do with it because it depends on how you see your future because actions you make in the present come from that vision, think for example when choosing a course of study: the first thing we think is “Will it be helpful when I finish it?
Will it help me find work?
Will it help me have a better life?” So, it is very important that we have the consciousness of what we are experiencing, if it is true that “we are living the best era in the history of humanity”, not of the last 50-100 years, but of humanity.
I am passionate about coaching. I know many coaches, But it was all invented by Socrates. Socrates was the only real coach, then we prepare slides
by changing the things he said, but this is his reasoning.
I just want to make people think in order to have a different vision.
First of all, let's clear the field from any possible misunderstanding: I am not mad at pessimists.
Pessimists and optimists make their contribution to the world;
thanks to optimists and to those who dreamed of flying the plane was invented, while pessimists have devised the parachute, and it is crucial that they have done it.
It is true that if you parachute you have to be optimistic, because then... Well, let's stop here.
What is our vision of the world?
If we look at the world as we have always seen it, for example on a geographical map, what is the idea we have of this world?
With what eyes are we looking at it?
Maybe someone here, an expert in geography, knows that this geographical map is not completely correct, and somehow reflects the area where the first maps were built, indeed, some continents are not the size they really are.
There is a map, called Peters map, which is more faithful to the size of individual continents.
Can you see Italy on this map?
Even those in the loggia who paid little?
Do you also see from above?
Well, those who do not see can zoom... And now ?
Well, we just need a few more seconds.
Did you find it?
We can't wait for everyone... Well, there is Italy, it is just turned.
This map is reversed, but actually this is the geographical map the primary school students have in their school books in Australia.
So, who is upside down?
The kangaroos or us?
Neither them nor us, but it is the vision that is different.
If we get a little bit out of our usual way of seeing things, maybe we can pick up some of the things we might miss.
We are living a revolution.
There have been many revolutions in the world, such as the Industrial Revolution, just think about the First Industrial Revolution, at the end of the 17th century: the steam engine, the loom, which completely changed not only the production processes but also the interpersonal relationships, the structure of society itself.
Two centuries later there was another revolution, called Second Industrial Revolution, which spread the assembly line and an extensive use of electricity, which changed further, causing traumas, fractures and subsequent well-being.
Think about the Third Industrial Revolution which I think we all have lived, with the advent of the Internet and then the last one we are experiencing now, which many define as the Fourth Industrial Revolution where we talk about genEtics and that capital E is not a typographical mistake, it gives the sense of new issues we will have to face.
Just think that Google is not hiring just IT engineers right now, it is hiring philosophers to figure out where our future world will go.
We are talking about 3D printing different connection speeds.
When we experience a revolution our first reaction is always fear. I remember my grandmother with the first cordless phone, it was a phone that no longer had the wire, she was worried and she thought “If words are not in there, where are they going?” Changes are worrying, even smaller ones.
An economist has written, I would like to read it with you, “We are suffering from an attack of pessimism.
Technical improvements have been proceeding at a greater rate in the last ten years than ever before in history.
The rapidity of this change is bringing difficult problems to solve, countries suffering the most are those not in the vanguard of progress.
We are being afflicted with a new disease: technological unemployment.
A form of unemployment caused by the fact that we find new ways to save work at a higher speed than that at which we find new ways to employ work.”
This could be an article of today but it was written in 1930.
And I do not need to tell you what happened in the decades that have elapsed since 1930.
It is just to let you know how much a change can create fears, often unfounded worries, if it is not correctly interpreted or not well framed.
One fear, the classic one, is that sooner or later progress will end.
This is the argument that pessimists, those of the parachute, obviously support: sooner or later progress will end.
In reality, we must think of progress as something accumulating not exhausting, something adding to discovered things, inventions, capabilities that we had before, something that adds up, not ends.
Obviously, progress can be divided into different areas of life and then we see that there are different rhythms.
While technological progress is under everyone's eyes, as if it had a very steep curve, social progress, not to mention the progress at the political level, are slower because this is precisely the characteristic of this era.
The characteristic of this era is speed.
Changes are so fast that our brain struggles to interpret and realize them, and for this reason we think that things cannot change quickly for the better.
Actually, technological progress is offering us incredible solutions for the well-being of everyone.
There are thousands of examples, look at the graph of the cost of the human genome mapping: 100 million dollars to make a human genome mapping in the 2000s.
100 million dollars. It could be afforded by the 2-3 richest people in the world.
A few years later it dropped with a very optimistic forecast following the Moore's law, according to which the performance of microprocessors are doubling every year.
So, it was a very positive forecast, “We will soon get from 100 million to 10 million” means that instead of the two richest people in the world, many other millionaires will be able to afford it.
Then an amazing thing has happened, not even predicted by the most optimistic scientists. This price has dropped very fast and the mapping, which cost 100 million dollars a few years ago, today costs less than a tablet, it is within the reach of many people.
What happened?
Studies by Frederick Sanger's, one of the pioneers in this industry, the fourth person in the world winning a Nobel Prize for Chemistry, were put online.
We moved from a series of concentrated intelligences, from 5, 10, 15 researchers closed in a laboratory, to a 5, 10, 15, 50 thousand researchers around the world, and this has accelerated the ability to reason on this issue, providing far cheaper and more affordable solutions.
For example, one of the extraordinary features our future will have is that it will be completely transparent.
I know it is provocative to talk about transparency here, but it will be so.
This is a bus stop in Singapore.
With cigarette butts taken from the ground, through DNA traces, the profiles of people who had thrown them have been reconstructed.
People took the picture from this profile, they put it on the bus stop and wrote “If you see this dirty person, tell him that cigarette butts must not be thrown on the ground.”
This is the transparency we are seeing in so many things, even in politics, in communication, and, as we said before, we cannot erase things from the internet and then they become transparent, a world where we control and we are increasingly playing a leading role.
Obviously, it is difficult to perceive this because maybe we see that there are taxi strikes in Rome or Milan, and we did not see that in July the first drone taxi was inaugurated in Dubai – it travels alone and is handled with a joystick.
You may say, “I would never get in there,” but it's the same thing we said the first time we took a plane.
We will use it because it will work.
There are paradoxical aspects, perhaps we may think that today's great feature is that we have generations of people glued to a smartphone screen, and it is true that China and now Belgium have devised special lanes on the sidewalks for people who are chatting.
So, one might even say, “This progress does not really seem so clever.”
Actually, there are things behind this progress that should make us think, for example when we talk about 5G.
Those with 4G are probably thinking “How will the 5G be?
The connection will be a bit faster, I will be able to download for example a movie - I mean legally - very quickly, so once I pay it I will download 1 giga in seven or eight seconds.
That's wonderful!”
Actually, it's not just that, because this only changes an evening when you have nothing to do at home.
Actually, 5G networks will have a wider and more powerful capacity than the optical fibre, so they will eventually allow automatic driving of cars, to operate remotely more easily and with even more precision will enter our lives the Internet of things will be a reality because these objects will interact with each other at unthinkable speeds.
If you are not optimistic with all we have, it is because you probably lack data.
Today the irrational person, the person looking to the future, is the pessimist.
And what do we teach our children?
What can we teach our children if statistics and research tell us that 65% of primary school students will do a job that does not exist today?
What can we teach them?
I believe we can teach them, and we can learn, to be better people optimistic people or those who transmit positive energy, these people have common features and I would like to briefly analyze them with you.
First of all, they are honest people – this is a revolutionary word at this time – they are normal people, they do not have a paresis.
No!
Well, an optimist person has ups and downs, has a different point of view.
It is true that they often smile optimistic people often smile and then they have the ability to share their good mood with other people, they never stop to make plans. Optimistic people never stop to make plans
whether they are 20, 30, or 70 years old.
They have a unique and extraordinary feature: they emphasize positive things.
We all have negative things in our lives and probably if we do a list they come out right away.
But how much would it take to make a list of positive things in our lives?
But if you have a parent, a son, a friend who loves you, a hospital within a 50-km radius, electricity, drinking water… They are things that should not be taken for granted.
Obviously, a person cannot come home, open the tap and say “Hurray to aqueduct society!”,
it would be an exaggeration, but you have to realize that this makes you a lucky person.
People who transmit positive energy are generous people who help others, this is what we can teach and what we can practice.
They do not have prejudices, and they are not afraid to transmit, and to show their affection.
So, today being optimistic maybe means just one thing: believe in people again, not because man is able to create a 5G network or to transport passengers with a drone, but because man is able to make the most wonderful thing that has ever appeared on this Earth: another human being.
And a person with this ability obviously makes you think positively about the future.
So, what can I do to change the world?
There are many who want to change the world, few think about how to change themselves.
I believe that if we really want to change the world, we just need to be the best version of ourselves.
Thank you (Applause)
